# ⛔⛔ THE BINARY'S NAME, AND IT WAS WRONG FOR AS LONG AS THE RENAME HAS BEEN
# DONE. These lines said /pkgm and /pkgm.*, which is what the command used to be
# called. After the rename to `zpkgm` nothing ignored the built binary any more,
# and a 14 MB arm64 Mach-O executable went into the repository and is in HEAD
# (blob e2ec8ed, 14 204 658 bytes). A rename is not finished when the code
# compiles: every file that names the old thing by spelling rather than by
# meaning stops working silently, and an ignore rule is the quietest of them.
#
# ⛔⛔ SO EVERY SPELLING STAYS, AND THE RENAME TO `zpkm` ADDS ONE RATHER THAN
# REPLACING ONE. The same thing happened a second time in this organisation on
# 2026-11-13 (go-zpkg/packages#449 listed only the CURRENT names while the
# committed files carried the old ones, and 29 MB stayed in HEAD while the
# pull request said they were gone). An ignore rule written from the name a
# thing SHOULD have cannot see the name it HAS — and after a rename a working
# tree holds BOTH, because the previous build is still sitting there.
/zpkm
/zpkm.*
/zpkgm
/zpkgm.*
/pkgm
/pkgm.*
*.out
bsd.test
bsd-tests.list
